                            <td>2010s</td>
                            <td>7.7</td>
                            <td class="highlight-green">-9.6%</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td>2020s</td>
                            <td>7.7</td>
                            <td>+0.2%</td>
                        </tr>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3>Key Observations</h3>
                    <ul>
                        <li>The <strong>1990s peak</strong> followed the dissolution of the Soviet Union and associated instability in Eastern Europe</li>
                        <li>The <strong>2000s saw marginal increase</strong> driven largely by violence in the Americas and parts of Africa</li>
                        <li>The <strong>2010s marked improvement</strong>, with many countries implementing better crime prevention policies</li>
                        <li><strong>2020s rates remain stable</strong>, though COVID-19 may have affected reporting quality</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="chart2_coverage.png" alt="Reporting Coverage by Year">
                <p class="chart-title">Figure 2: Number of countries reporting homicide data per year. Coverage peaked around 2019 with 137 countries, and has declined to 84 in 2023.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="callout warning">
                <strong>Data Coverage Note:</strong> Reporting coverage varies significantly across years. While peak coverage reached 162 countries (2008), the most recent year (2023) only has data from 84 countries. This declining coverage affects the representativeness of recent global estimates.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- REGIONAL COMPARISON -->
        <section id="regional-comparison">
            <h2>2. Regional & Country-Level Comparison</h2>
            <p>Geographic analysis reveals profound disparities in homicide rates across world regions. The Americas consistently record the highest rates, while Europe and Asia demonstrate significantly lower levels of lethal violence.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="chart3_regional.png" alt="Regional Homicide Rate Trends">
                <p class="chart-title">Figure 3: Regional median homicide rate trends. The Americas show persistently high rates, while Europe and Asia have maintained much lower levels throughout the observation period.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="two-col">
                <div>
                    <h3>Highest Homicide Rates (2023)</h3>
                    <table>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Country</th>
                            <th>Rate</th>
                        </tr>
                        <tr class="highlight-red">
                            <td>Saint Kitts and Nevis</td>
                            <td>64.2</td>
                        </tr>
