            </div>

            <div class="callout danger">
                <strong>Critical Disparity:</strong> The gap between the highest and lowest homicide rates spans two orders of magnitude—from 64.2 (Saint Kitts and Nevis) to near-zero (Holy See, Singapore). This disparity reflects differences in governance, socioeconomic factors, drug trafficking routes, and institutional capacity.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SEX DISPARITY -->
        <section id="sex-disparity">
            <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>
            <p>One of the most consistent findings across all countries and time periods is the significant gender disparity in homicide victimization. Males face a substantially higher risk of being homicide victims than females, with the global male-to-female homicide ratio remaining remarkably stable around 3:1 to 4:1.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="chart5_gender.png" alt="Gender Disparity in Homicide Over Time">
                <p class="chart-title">Figure 4: Male-to-female homicide rate ratio over time. The ratio has remained consistently between 2.5 and 4.0, indicating persistent gender asymmetry in violence victimization.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="kpi-grid">
                <div class="kpi-card green">
                    <div class="kpi-label">Global Male-to-Female Ratio (2023)</div>
                    <div class="kpi-value">3.18x</div>
                    <div class="kpi-label">Males are 3x more likely to be homicide victims</div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <h3>Interpretation</h3>
            <p>The persistent male predominance in homicide victimization reflects multiple factors:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Armed conflict and violence:</strong> Young males are disproportionately involved in interpersonal violence, gang activity, and armed conflicts</li>
                <li><strong>Risk-taking behavior:</strong> Studies indicate higher rates of risk-taking behavior among males, particularly in adolescence and young adulthood</li>
                <li><strong>Substance abuse:</strong> Alcohol and drug involvement is more strongly associated with male violent crime</li>
                <li><strong>Economic marginalization:</strong> Young males in disadvantaged communities face higher homicide risk</li>
            </ul>

            <div class="callout info">
                <strong>Important Note:</strong> While males face higher absolute homicide rates, females are not immune to lethal violence. Intimate partner homicide remains a significant issue globally, and gender-based killing of women and girls (femicid/feminicide) has gained increasing attention as a distinct phenomenon requiring targeted policy responses.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- AGE GROUP ANALYSIS -->
        <section id="age-group-analysis">
            <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>
            <p>Homicide risk varies significantly across age groups, with young adults (ages 15-44) consistently bearing the heaviest burden of lethal violence worldwide. This pattern holds across virtually all countries with available data.</p>

            <div class="chart-container">
                <img src="chart6_age.png" alt="Homicide Rates by Age Group and Sex">
                <p class="chart-title">Figure 5: Homicide rates by age group for males and females (most recent available year). Young adults aged 20-29 face the highest homicide risk, with rates declining progressively in older age groups.</p>
            </div>

            <table>
                <tr>
